\chapter*{Preface}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Preface}

One end of this story is a phototypesetter. The other is a phone. Between
them lie minicomputers, university computer rooms, engineering workstations,
Internet servers, and machines small enough to disappear inside other
products. A system made to provide a congenial place for programming became
an infrastructure for people who would never willingly type a shell command.
That change is the subject of this book.

The C/A/T in the title is the Graphic Systems phototypesetter used at Bell
Labs. It is not the \texttt{cat} command, although that small command belongs
to the same story. Typesetting made Unix useful beyond the circle of its
implementers. Android demonstrates a different kind of reach: a Unix-like
kernel can underpin an environment whose everyday interface scarcely
resembles a time-sharing terminal. Neither endpoint was inevitable.

This is both a history and an explanation of working machinery. The first
part follows the formation and spread of Unix. The second opens the kernel,
using John Lions's Sixth Edition commentary and the supplied October 2019
RISC-V edition of xv6 as deliberately different specimens. The third studies
programming tools and visual interfaces. The fourth follows Unix ideas into
servers, games, and phones. Chapters can be read separately, but earlier
distinctions between an interface, an implementation, and an institution
make the later chapters easier to understand.

The intended reader is curious about computing and comfortable encountering
small fragments of shell and C. No prior kernel course is assumed. The
examples explain a mechanism rather than offer production libraries. Where
an example omits error handling or describes pseudocode, the text says so.
The practical investigations in the appendices use temporary data and do
not require administrative privileges.

The supplied \texttt{sources/} collection is the historical and technical
foundation. It contains duplicates, empty files, OCR errors, translated
editions, and filenames that do not reliably identify their contents. The
bibliography and source notes identify the actual works used. Citations
prefer section names when extraction has made page numbers unreliable.
All narrative, comparisons, and worked examples here are newly written;
this book does not reproduce the supplied books or their source listings.

Later developments require additional explanation. The chapters on Wayland,
containers, Android, and gaming combine established systems knowledge with
the official project documentation identified in the bibliography. This is
a structural account, not a release catalogue or a market-share survey.
The modern discussion has an editorial cutoff of 6 November 2026; it makes
no claim that a particular game, device, or distribution behaves identically
across versions.

Several words need care. \emph{Unix} names a historical family and a design
tradition. \emph{UNIX} also names a trademark tied to certification.
\emph{Linux} strictly names a kernel, although people commonly use it for
complete systems assembled around that kernel. \emph{Free software} concerns
user freedoms; \emph{open source} concerns an overlapping licensing and
development movement. These meanings intersect without becoming synonyms.

There is no single protagonist. Thompson and Ritchie matter, as do Ossanna,
McIlroy, Cherry, Kernighan, the Berkeley groups, Stallman, Torvalds, and many
less visible maintainers and users. The recurring actor is the interface:
the agreement that lets one person's work become another person's building
material. An interface can preserve freedom, conceal complexity, transmit a
mistake, or harden into a barrier. Following those agreements helps explain
how Unix endured while so much of its original machinery disappeared.
